<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Nathan Tracey — a Sussex Police Inspector who came to policing the long way round, and writes here about evidence, curiosity, and the work."
>
  <article>
    <header class="border-b border-ink-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-10 py-14 md:py-20">
        <p class="font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent mb-5">
          About
        </p>
        <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold text-ink-900 leading-[1.05] tracking-tight">
          About this site, and the person writing it.
        </h1>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-10 py-12 md:py-16">
      <div class="prose-article">
        <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-ink-800 leading-relaxed">
          I'm Nathan Tracey — a Police Inspector with Sussex Police, based in Hampshire, and the person typing all of this. This is where I think out loud about the work.
        </p>

        <img
          src="/images/nathan-tracey.jpg"
          alt="Nathan Tracey"
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full max-w-sm rounded-xl shadow-sm mx-auto my-10"
        />

        <h2>The long way round</h2>

        <p>
          I didn't achieve much at school. I spent years managing a department at Tesco, and I joined Sussex Police at thirty-one. It isn't the tidy graduate-fast-track story, and I've never wanted it to be — most of what I find interesting about policing happens to people who didn't take the tidy route either.
        </p>

        <p>
          At thirty-seven I was diagnosed with ADHD, and I don't say this for effect: it genuinely saved my life. Understanding how my own mind actually works — why I am the way I am — is the thing that let me build the life I've got. A surprising amount of what I write traces back to that diagnosis in the end: the gap between how people are assumed to function and how they really do, and what changes when you finally close it.
        </p>

        <h2>What actually drives me</h2>

        <p>
          In my police interview, years ago, I said the truest thing about myself before I'd worked out it was the truest thing about myself: I hate being bored, and I love being interested. I'm at my best with an idea to chew on — a project, a problem, a question about why the job is done the way it is. I think I'm a person with good values, but I don't think that lets me off the hook. I'd rather understand <em>why</em> I believe something than simply believe it, and I enjoy having my thinking challenged at least as much as I enjoy challenging everyone else's. This site is mostly that instinct with the volume turned up.
        </p>

        <h2>The day job, and the numbers</h2>

        <p>
          I work in neighbourhood policing, and I spend a fair amount of time on data alongside it — officer assaults, use of force, misconduct trends, driving standards. I'm drawn to early intervention: what we can do upstream of a problem rather than mopping up downstream of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          I didn't arrive at any of this through a statistics degree. It started, honestly, with the radio — Tim Harford's <a href="/other/more-or-less-and-the-art-of-not-being-fooled-by-numbers/">More or Less</a> and a steady diet of BBC podcasts — and grew from there into <a href="/influential-people/jerry-ratcliffe-and-the-case-for-intelligence-led-policing/">Jerry Ratcliffe's</a> work on intelligence-led policing. Closer to home it owes a great deal to the example of Superintendent Ben Newman at Sussex, and to the support of <a href="/influential-people/alex-murray-and-the-rise-of-evidence-based-policing/">Alex Murray</a>, who founded the Society of Evidence-Based Policing. Between them they convinced me of something I now can't unsee: that an officer's instinct is a hypothesis, not a verdict — and that checking it is the interesting part, not the dull bit at the end.
        </p>

        <h2>The puzzle on the side</h2>

        <p>
          And then there's the <a href="/other/cracking-the-somerton-man-code/">Somerton Man</a>. I first heard about the coded note years ago and I've come back to it ever since — a constant, nagging mystery that I've always been certain has a human answer rather than a mystical one. I haven't solved it. But I've got the quiet feeling that I'm close, and for a brain like mine that's almost the better place to be.
        </p>

        <h2>What this site is</h2>

        <p>
          Thinking About Policing is a place for evidence-based writing on policing and public policy. It's aimed at senior police leaders, policymakers, academics, and frontline officers who want to read something with a bit more thought behind it than the usual commentary.
        </p>

        <p>
          The pieces here tend to share a few things in common. They start from data or research where it exists. They acknowledge limits where the evidence is contested. And they try to land a point, rather than just survey the field.
        </p>

        <h2>Three sections</h2>

        <p>
          The writing falls into three rough buckets.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Police Policy</strong> covers the operational and organisational side of policing. Misconduct prevention, officer wellbeing, use of force, neighbourhood policing, evidence-based practice. The stuff that affects how the job is actually done.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Public Policy</strong> is wider. Criminal justice, social policy, the political context that shapes what police are asked to do. Sometimes the most useful policing reform isn't a policing reform at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Interesting Digressions</strong> is for the occasional piece that doesn't fit the two policy sections — the Somerton Man code, an appreciation of <em>More or Less</em>, and whatever else the restless brain wanders into.
        </p>

        <h2>A note on views</h2>

        <p>
          Everything here is written in a personal capacity. The views are mine and don't represent Sussex Police, any other employer, or any organisation I'm associated with. Where I draw on research, I'll cite it. Where I'm offering a personal read, I'll say so.
        </p>

        <h2>Get in touch</h2>

        <p>
          The comments on each article don't require a login, so please join the conversation — questions, challenges, and different perspectives are all welcome. If you'd rather email, you can reach me at <a href="mailto:ntracey@gmail.com">ntracey@gmail.com</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
